{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774741,"nonce":"ry6ahZKfk9zYtC+Bv4MzAR4DQa4=","title":"Close the last three open tickets: failed-attempt artifacts, App preflight, real-Forgejo verification","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #85 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `integrate/open-issues-2026-07-26` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-26T02:25:26Z · Merged: 2026-07-26T03:08:23Z\n\nCloses the three tickets that were open in git-bug: `a28a173`, `90644c6`, `3863d5e`.\n\n\n`drive_workspace` gated the harvest on `exit == 0`, so the scheduler's `ExecState::Failed` harvest branch (commented \"often THE evidence\") was dead code. A failing test's JUnit XML, a crashed build's log bundle, a screenshot from a failed browser run — all silently discarded.\n\nThe harvest is now owed for any terminated step until the index annotation lands on the Pod. Rather than adding a second barrier, this **reuses the one #78 introduced**: `settled_state` reports `Running` for `Failed { class: Step }` while the index is still outstanding. It is deliberately narrower than the Succeeded withhold — a failed step's workspace is not an output, so it is withheld only while the *index* is outstanding, and a wedged sidecar cannot hide a verdict already known. `Infra`/`Timeout`/`Config`/`Lost` pass through verbatim.\n\nA harvest error is logged as evidence and returned as `DriveErr::Transient` only, never `OutputContract`/`InputMissing`, so it can never surface as `Failed{Config}`/`Failed{Infra}`. The class is re-derived from the Pod exit code each poll, so a broken harvest can delay the report but never change it.\n\n\nA GitHub App can be misconfigured in two ways that both fail *silently*: an empty webhook-event subscription (`/v1/repos` looks healthy while no run ever triggers) and a missing `statuses:write` grant (every status post 403s while the run goes green).\n\n`GET /v1/connections/{id}/preflight` mints an installation token and diffs the App's actual granted permissions and subscribed events against what Scarab needs. The required set lives in **one place** (`crates/scarab-forge/src/preflight.rs`) as static tables keyed by `ForgeKind`, each entry carrying a severity and a \"what silently breaks\" sentence; the diff is pure and fails closed on an unknown level. The actual half is a new `ForgePort::describe_capabilities` (defaults to `Unsupported`), implemented for GitHub over `GET /app`.\n\nIt answers **`200` with status `unknown`** rather than an error when the forge cannot be introspected or the credential does not resolve — \"could not look\" must not render as healthy, which is the same trap the endpoint exists to catch. Surfaced in Settings directly under the existing credential health line. No credential material in the response (asserted).\n\n\nEvery shape in the Forgejo path was asserted against unit tests of shapes we *believed* Forgejo uses, and one of those guesses had already shipped (`register_webhook` sent no `config.secret`, so every delivery would 401 against our own endpoint).\n\n`just forgejo-verify` now stands up a real `codeberg.org/forgejo/forgejo:16.0.1`, seeds an admin + token + 57 repos (enough to force a second API page), points the proc stack at it, and drives add-connection → pick-list → bind → hook registered → real push → Run. Env-gated on `SCARAB_TEST_FORGEJO=1` exactly like the `SCARAB_TEST_KUBE` tier; CI runs neither.\n\n\n1. **Duplicate hooks.** `Hook.url` is top-level while `Hook.config` is documented deprecated; our idempotency check read only `config.url`, so re-registration created a second hook.\n2. **Silently truncated pick-list.** Paging stopped on a *short* page rather than an empty one. Forgejo emits short pages whenever `MAX_RESPONSE_ITEMS` is below our page size.\n3. **`SCARAB_PUBLIC_URL` was ignored for webhook registration.** `AppState.public_url` was only ever assigned by `with_oauth_login`, so with browser login disabled every hook Scarab registered pointed at the hardcoded `http://localhost:8080` — registration reporting success while delivery was structurally impossible. **This affected Helm deployments, not just this tier.** `with_oauth_login` loses the parameter entirely so it cannot be re-coupled, and `try_register_webhook` now warns when a callback resolves to loopback.\n4. **`up.sh` could succeed against a server it did not start.** A stale orphan on 8080 (`down.sh` kills only the pidfile pid, so a second `up.sh` orphans the first) made the tier drive the *old binary* through two full false-red runs — and could as easily have produced a false **green**, which would make the tier worthless as evidence. `up.sh` now claims the port before building, refuses a listener it does not own with an actionable message, and proves its child is alive before declaring the stack up.\n\nDefects 3 and 4 are the same shape as the `config.secret` bug that motivated the ticket: **an operation that reports success while being structurally incapable of working.**\n\nAlso fixed: the compose DB path (`/data/forgejo` is outside the only subtree the image entrypoint chowns, so Forgejo died in a DB-init retry loop before ever binding), and the scenario is re-runnable — it reclaims a connection left by an earlier run (single-owner precedence correctly 409s) and requires a *new* Run id, since durable Runs from a previous invocation would otherwise satisfy the poll and read green.\n\n\nRun once against the merged result, including `origin/main`'s OAuth hardening (#84):\n\n| Tier | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `just check` | clean |\n| `just test` | **671 passed**, 22 skipped |\n| UI vitest / UI build | **72 passed** / clean |\n| kind tier (`SCARAB_TEST_KUBE=1`, 16 tests) | **16 passed** — incl. the new `artifacts_are_harvested_from_a_failed_step` and the cancel / deadline / rerun-supersede cases that traverse the modified `settled_state` |\n| `just e2e` | **5 passed** |\n| `just forgejo-verify` | **exit 0** — all three live tests green against a real Forgejo |\n\nThe `up.sh` guard was verified positively by squatting port 8080 with a foreign listener: exit 1 with an actionable message.\n\n\n- The App preflight has **never been exercised against a real GitHub App** — the failure mode is not reproducible offline and not k8s-observable. `describe_capabilities` parses a real `GET /app` body in unit tests only.\n- Forgejo has no capability-introspection API, so its preflight always answers `unknown`; its requirement table is documentation-only.\n- `contract_live.rs` is excluded from the recipe — its `list_dir_at_ref` fixture uses `dir: \"\"` and may 404 on a real instance, reddening the tier for an unrelated reason. The one-liner to run it is in `deploy/local-forgejo/README.md`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}